INFERENCE WEEKLY
Friday, February 27, 2026

Hello. The week in running models, for people who pay the bill.

IN PRODUCTION: Sable-3 Mini
Three days after release, it is already the default routing target at two of the larger inference gateways for classification and extraction traffic. Early reports: latency to first token around 180ms at p50, quality on par with last year's mid-tier, and the price is the price. One team told us they moved 40% of their volume to it in an afternoon by changing a routing rule.

COST CORNER: long context is not free, even when it is cached
A 200K-token context at current input prices is about $0.40 per request on a frontier model before caching and $0.06 after. That is fine at a thousand requests a day and ruinous at a million. Retrieval is not dead; it just moved from "necessary" to "cheaper."

FIVE LINKS
1. A clear write-up of how continuous batching actually works, with diagrams.
2. The serving framework release that finally makes multi-LoRA serving stable.
3. A benchmark of six embedding models on industrial-document retrieval. The small ones win.
4. A post-mortem from a team whose test set leaked into their fine-tuning data. Honest and useful.
5. The talk from last week's systems conference on KV-cache compression, now with slides.

ONE NUMBER: 2.6x. Median throughput gain from turning on speculative decoding for chat-shaped traffic, across the twelve deployments readers sent us this week.

That's the week. Reply with what you are running.

